<HowtoLayout slug="holiday-visuals-from-product-photos" ctaLabel="Create your account" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">The Black Friday scene is the easy part. What goes wrong is the product, and the price written across it. Change the room and keep the product exactly as shot. The offer goes on last, on top, where it can still change.</p>

  <h2>How do you make Black Friday product images with AI from photos you already have?</h2>

  <p>Use your existing packshot as the source of an image edit. The prompt names what changes (scene, props, light) and what must not (label, color, shape). Check every render against the packshot, add the offer as text on top rather than inside the picture, and confirm the price claim. hubStudio does each step in one app.</p>

  <p>The sequence, start to finish:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pick the sources.</strong> One sharp packshot per product, plus a close crop of the label.</li>
    <li><strong>Write one scene prompt.</strong> It says what changes and what stays, and it asks for empty space where the offer will sit.</li>
    <li><strong>Run it on several engines at once</strong>, each run in its own tab.</li>
    <li><strong>Check every render against the packshot.</strong> A wrong label means a rerun, not a retouch.</li>
    <li><strong>Add the offer in the Image editor</strong>, as text over the picture, on a copy.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the price claim</strong> against the rule of the market it runs in.</li>
    <li><strong>Frame each copy for its placement</strong> and keep the launch together in one campaign.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When is Black Friday 2026, and what else falls in the season?</h2>

  <p>Black Friday is Friday, Nov. 27, 2026, the day after Thanksgiving. Cyber Monday follows on Nov. 30.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Thanksgiving falls on Thursday, Nov. 26, 2026, and Christmas on Friday, Dec. 25.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US Office of Personnel Management, federal holidays 2026, read Oct. 19, 2026. Government holiday list.</p>

