  <h2>How do you write a prompt that changes the scene and not the product?</h2>

  <p>Write an edit instruction, not a description of a new picture. Give the scene its sentences, then give the product a full sentence of its own: what it is, its colors, the exact words on its label in quotation marks. Engine makers say the same.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Google's image generation documentation tells users that to preserve critical details such as a logo during an edit, they should "describe them in great detail along with your edit request."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Gemini API image generation documentation, the maker's own page, last updated Oct. 9, 2026, read Oct. 19, 2026.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Part of the prompt</th>
          <th scope="col">Changes or stays</th>
          <th scope="col">Example wording</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Scene</th>
          <td>Changes</td>
          <td>"on a dark walnut table beside a window, early winter evening"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Props</th>
          <td>Changes</td>
          <td>"two pine sprigs and a folded wool scarf, cropped by the frame"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Light</th>
          <td>Changes</td>
          <td>"one warm lamp from the left, one soft shadow to the right"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Product shape</th>
          <td>Stays</td>
