  <h2>What rules apply to the sale price on a holiday visual?</h2>

  <p>The overlay makes a price claim, and in the US and the EU that claim has to stand on the price you actually charged before.</p>

  <p>In the US, the reference is the Federal Trade Commission's guidance on former price comparisons: the "was" in a was-and-now offer.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A former price supports a price comparison only if it is "the actual, bona fide price at which the article was offered to the public on a regular basis for a reasonably substantial period of time."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: FTC Guides Against Deceptive Pricing, 16 CFR 233.1(a), eCFR text current as of Oct. 15, 2026. Federal guidance under section 5 of the FTC Act.</p>

  <p>The same section adds that a visual saying only "Sale" still promises a real saving: the cut must not be "so insignificant as to be meaningless." The guides set no fixed number of days for the former price.</p>

  <p>The EU does set one.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"Any announcement of a price reduction shall indicate the prior price," meaning "the lowest price applied by the trader during a period of time not shorter than 30 days prior to the application of the price reduction."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Directive (EU) 2019/2161, Article 6a of Directive 98/6/EC, applied from May 28, 2022. EU legislation; member states may adjust it for perishable goods, new products and progressive reductions.</p>

  <p>In production terms: the person who owns the price history signs off the number before the overlay is typed, and for an EU market the announcement carries the prior price, so leave room for it in the overlay. Write both prices and the period into the campaign's brief, so whoever makes the Cyber Monday copy reads the same figures. This describes production practice, not legal advice.</p>

